scope: src/service/buildBrainState
status: misaligned
description: >
  Read-only brain-state detector — consumes `.aide/config/brain.aide` via
  parseBrainAide as the source of truth, returns `no-mcp-entry` immediately
  when any element of the parsed `mcpServerConfig.args` is null (a
  null-bearing config is by definition not a wireable launch command),
  otherwise compares the parsed `mcpServerConfig` against the host's
  `.mcp.json` brain entry for drift, and returns a closed four-state tagged
  union (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`) that the boot
  probe and runtime brain tool both consume without re-deriving detection
  logic. The null-args structural check runs BEFORE the byte-for-byte
  drift comparison, so a null-bearing `brain.aide` matched byte-for-byte
  by an equally null-bearing `.mcp.json` does not slip through as `ok`.
  The detector never stats a path; path validity is the launcher's problem
  at MCP server startup.
intent: >
  buildBrainState is the single canonical-code detector that resolves the
  host project's brain precondition state into a structured `BrainState`
  tagged union drawn from a closed four-state vocabulary — `ok`,
  `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`. The detector's job is
  structural classification of the brain wiring state. It calls
  `parseBrainAide(root)` to read the user-owned source of truth
  (`.aide/config/brain.aide`); if any element of the parsed
  `mcpServerConfig.args` is null, the detector returns `no-mcp-entry`
  immediately — null in args is a structural property of "wiring not
  done," and a null-armed launch command is unwireable regardless of
  what `.mcp.json` contains. Otherwise it reads `.mcp.json`, looks up
  the fixed `brain` key, and structurally compares the parsed
  `mcpServerConfig` against the actual `.mcp.json` brain entry. Every
  failure mode collapses into the closed four-state vocabulary — no
  fifth state for null-armed args, because null-bearing configs collapse
  semantically into `no-mcp-entry` (the brain's MCP entry is not yet
  wireable, which is exactly what `no-mcp-entry` already means). The
  detector is read-only: it never writes `.mcp.json`, never auto-syncs
  on drift, never spawns processes, never stats any path on disk, never
  throws. It also never imports from a backend registry, never branches
  on the user's `name` label, never re-implements brain.aide parsing,
  and never pattern-matches string contents to detect placeholders —
  `name` is descriptive metadata threaded through unchanged, the parser
  owns the file-format contract, path validation is the launcher's job,
  and YAML null is the unambiguous unwired-slot signal (no literal
  sentinel like `<BRAIN_PATH>` is recognized). The default scaffolded
  brain.aide carries the brain's location inline in `mcpServerConfig.args`
  byte-for-byte for fully-wired slots, with no `${...}` placeholders to
  interpolate; the parser still applies `${...}` substitution if any
  frontmatter field is referenced (advanced users may DRY a value across
  positions), but with only `name` available to substitute, that surface
  is essentially dormant in the default flow. Two distinct consumers
  depend on this module: the runtime brain tool (`aide_brain`) composes
  its agent-facing prose from the resolved state, and the boot probe
  (`aide_info`) gates pipeline work on it. Both receive the same
  plain-data state with the same discriminant rules.
